      "gap": "Partial: the source's complete argument is not reconstructed at four points. (1) Merin2021 p. 268, sec. 5.1: the case for the term 'relevance' has two grounds and a conclusion: 'confirmation', 'corroboration' and 'support' need the prefix 'incremental' not to be read as P(H|E) (supplied at inductive-logic-and-confirmation-w02b-p15-025.tex lines 81-83); they 'sound awkward with the prefix negative' (absent); and 'relevance' (Keynes 1921, Carnap 1950, Jeffrey 2004) has neither problem (absent: w02b-p15-024 lines 1007-1008 list the three only as users of the word). (2) p. 270: the claim that distinct but ordinally equivalent measures can differ in convenience of range and, more importantly for cognitive-engineering applications, in algebraic properties is neither stated nor attributed (the KO/L/LL composition facts at w02b-p15-024 lines 201-235 illustrate an algebraic difference without stating Merin's claim or its weighting). (3) p. 266: extreme positivity is offered as what is conveyed by asserting 'If A, then B' without reservations, with the qualification that such an assertion can also be made with less certainty; rationality-4-3.tex lines 89-91 relate extreme positivity to indicative conditionals without the unreserved-assertion condition or the qualification. (4) Theorem 10(a), p. 273, promises the convex-combination identity for D and Q whenever P(AB)=0; rationality-4-3.tex lines 440-442 add P(A)+P(B)>0 and a nondegenerate target, and the excluded cases (degenerate target; P(A)=P(B)=0) are neither stated nor shown (thm:p15-w02b-p15-024-exneg-disjuncts item 3 covers only P(A)=0<P(B)). Owed: supply each of these under the source's actual assumptions, with explicit justification and locators."
